<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward the Confessor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1003–<a href="/years/1066">1066</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward the Confessor was king of the English from 1042 to 1066, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a>. Raised in exile in Normandy during the Danish rule of Cnut and his sons, he succeeded his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a>. He built the first great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> and was later canonised. His death without children led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>, as both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a> claimed the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor.jpg" alt="Edward the Confessor" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myrabella · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_Tapestry_scene1_EDWARD_REX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward was the seventh son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a> and the first by his second wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a>, and was born between 1003 and 1005 at Islip, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> seized the throne in 1013, Emma fled to Normandy with Edward and his brother Alfred, and in 1016 Cnut became undisputed king. Edward spent about twenty-five years in exile, probably mainly in Normandy. His mother married Cnut in 1017 and favoured her son by Cnut, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a>. In 1036 Alfred was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godwin-earl-of-wessex">Godwin, Earl of Wessex</a>, and died of his injuries, which is thought to be the source of much of Edward's hatred for Godwin. In 1041 Harthacnut, who had not long to live, invited Edward back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, probably as his heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a> died on 8 June 1042, Godwin supported Edward, who was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> on Easter Sunday, 3 April 1043. His position was weak, as he had to keep on terms with the three leading earls, Leofric of Mercia, Godwin and Siward of Northumbria, and had no personal power base. He married Godwin's daughter Edith on 23 January 1045. In 1051 a quarrel over the appointment of the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> and an affray at Dover led to the Godwins' flight, but they returned in 1052 with an army and Edward had to restore them.</p><p>From the later 1050s the Godwin brothers controlled nearly all the earldoms, and Edward seems to have withdrawn from active politics. Historians disagree about the reign: some see the decline of royal power, while the biographers Frank Barlow and Peter Rex portray a successful king. He had no children, and historians have long puzzled over his intentions for the succession. Edward died at Westminster on 5 January 1066 and was buried the next day, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> was crowned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Edward's major building project was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-architecture">Norman Romanesque</a> church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, consecrated on 28 December 1065; he was too weak to attend. Most historians regard him as an unlikely saint and his canonisation as political. Osbert of Clare, prior of Westminster, campaigned for it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> issued the bull on 7 February 1161. Edward was a national saint of England until about 1350, when Edward III preferred Saint George. His shrine in the abbey remains where Henry III placed it on 13 October 1269, the date of his feast.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward the Confessor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His greatest enterprise followed the death of <b>Edward the Confessor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Cnut and his sons ruled until 1042, when the old West Saxon line returned with <b>Edward the Confessor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Gruffudd’s death in 1063, Bleddyn ap Cynfyn and his brother Rhiwallon were set up as rulers of Gwynedd and Powys under the overlordship of <b>Edward the Confessor</b>, and Bleddyn’s descendants made Powys a separate kingdom again.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Edward the Confessor</b> of England had no children or clear successor, and succession was decided jointly by the king and the assembly of nobles, the Witenagemot.</blockquote><a href="/work/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…manor the record gives the tax assessment, the arable land and plough teams, meadow, woodland, pasture, fisheries, mills and salt-pans, the peasants by class, and the annual value at the death of <b>Edward the Confessor</b>, when the new holder received it and at the time of the survey.</blockquote><a href="/work/domesday-book">Domesday Book · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke William of Normandy defeated and killed Harold Godwinson at the Battle of Hastings on 14 October 1066, and on Christmas Day he was crowned king of England in Westminster Abbey, the church rebuilt by <b>Edward the Confessor</b> and consecrated only the year before.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Islip, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.82,-1.24&amp;year=1043" aria-label="Islip on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4522 372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward the Confessor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4972 642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4972 642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.82,-1.24&amp;year=1043">What was here in 1043? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
